{"name":"Gold Leaf","hex":"#edbb26","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold leaf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Gold that is beaten into a thin sheet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold leaf","blocks":[{"text":"Gold leaf is gold that has been hammered into thin sheets by a process known as goldbeating, for use in gilding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/330px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_leaf","wikidata":"Q929186"},{"title":"Gold Leaf (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold Leaf is a 2021 Taiwanese period drama based on the eponymous novel written by Huang Kuo-hua. Set in the 1950s, Gold Leaf is Taiwan's first Hailu Hakka language drama."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Leaf_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q109849625"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/500px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/1280px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg/500px-Gilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Mycenaean necklace; 1400–1050 BC; gilded terracotta; diameter of the rosettes: 2.7 cm (1.1 in), with variations of about 0.1 cm (0.04 in), and a pendant length of 3.7 cm (1.5 in); Metropolitan Museum of Art (New York City)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGilt_terracotta_ornaments_from_a_necklace_MET_DP145718.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg/500px-Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg/500px-Goldbeating_-_Weigel_%285301539%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An engraving showing the goldbeating process, 1698","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoldbeating_-_Weigel_(5301539).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Goldleaf.jpg/500px-Goldleaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Goldleaf.jpg/500px-Goldleaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"22k gold leaf applied with an ox hair brush during the process of gilding","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoldleaf.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Hanna_book_cover.jpg/500px-Hanna_book_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Hanna_book_cover.jpg/1280px-Hanna_book_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Hanna_book_cover.jpg/500px-Hanna_book_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early 20th century leather book cover, with gold leaf orn